e assumption that something would  indeed be easy and this is what uh is  important and what we've been discussing  and i wanted to ask about uh abortion uh  yesterday minister ian  said that you're keeping all of your  options on the table with regards to  protecting the ability to obtain an  abortion some have raised concerns that  legislation could in fact have the  opposite effect in some respects and i'm  wondering as you're considering whether  to legislate or not what are the factors  that you're currently taking into  consideration  obviously  the primary factor we take into  consideration is  every woman in canada should have a full  access to legal  safe  abortion services  and reproductive health services  wherever she is in the country  and  that these gains that we have that now  that right now for everyone in the  country not be rolled back by future  governments or  future decisions  the best way to do that as you've  pointed out there are discussions well  maybe it's legislation maybe it's not  legislation maybe it's leaving it in the  hands of the canadian medical  association that has ensured governance  over over these procedures for a long  time whereas a government like ours has  continued to as well  make real investments and  hold  provincial health systems to account  where they haven't been delivering uh  access proper access to people uh to to  women across the country so  there's lots to do there's lots to  continue to do that we have been doing  and i think this is a moment where  the  threat and the potential of an  overturning of roe versus wade in the  united states  has women  in canada indeed around the world very  worried that there is going to be a  rolling back of  significant gains that they have made  that we have all made in women's rights  so yes people are asking a lot of  questions about it people are very  concerned about it and i am  pleased to be able to repeat once again  that our government is unequivocal that  women have the freedom to choose and we  will  do  everything we can to ensure that that's  not a freedom that can be rolled back by  any future government last question from  evan good afternoon prime minister my  question first question is for you uh  today your country's parliament approved  the removal of a soviet war monument in  riga uh and i know that your country has  probably the largest russian population  ethnic russian population in nato could  you just tell us a bit about how the  ukraine war has affected unity in your  country how it's affected your russian  population and what it brought to latvia  all right thank you very much for the  question  uh so uh  the ukraine war  has changed  not only everything in the world also  the situation in our own country uh we  have a legacy of many russian speakers  in our country uh from the 1970s and  1980s in the process of of being  occupied were also in the being  russified and uh about 700 000 russian  migrants were moved russian-speaking  migrants were moved to latvia in that  time  since regaining independence the the the  proportion of of latvian speakers is  changing the entire situation in our  country has changed but we still have a  minority of russian speakers whose  families came whether from russia or  ukraine or belarus or kazakhstan or  azerbaijani from all over the former  soviet union who until the war started  on on the whole had very sympathetic  views towards  putin and the kremlin  as a as a result of history  family ties  traditions etc  as the war  started  what we've seen in our country is a  reassessment  of the former allegiance or you know the  moral or allegiance towards russia and  towards the kremlin in light of of the  actual  atrocities that are occurring in ukraine  as we see in butch as we see in mario  paul as we see in  her son as we see all over  the areas where the russian military has  been or is currently occupying and  according to the uh the latest uh polls  uh that the the number or the percentage  of our population  that still supports uh putin  has been  going down quite drastically and of the  entire population it is somewhere around  a four or five percent  so it's actually a small minority that  still feels somehow that putin is to be  supported my uh  guess i guess that's the best way to say  is that as time goes on this percentage  will be decreasing still further  so  the legacy of occupation is difficult  for any country to overcome in our case  50 years of occupation and now 32 years  of re of being independent once again  is is a process that we're going through  but  what putin is effectively doing  is probably counter to anything that he  imagined one  he has solidified the european union  two he has rejuvenated and solidified us  as nato member states giving us new  purpose and bringing countries like  canada and latvia closer together and  three he is not  uh uh  bringing citizens apart in my country  but actually having more and more uh  citizens who've been living in latvia  for 32 years who still had the feeling  that somehow but but russia is i mean  russia is a great country but moscow is  a is a you know putin is a great  government  reassessing that and realizing that it's  not that way so it is a process and  actually putin is having an effect that  i think that he never imagined which is  re-solidifying the democratic world and  actually in a country such as ours uh to  create a new understanding of what it is  to be latvian he did this in the ukraine  eight years ago and in a sense through  his uh uh occupation and illegal  incorporation into into russia of crimea  and his ongoing war on donbass  he has  forged in many ways a strong ukrainian  nation which is no longer  only ethnically based but there are many  russian speaking ukrainians who are 100  percent loyal to ukraine who maybe 10  years ago if they would have been polled  would have had equally warm feelings  towards russia  so this is one of the unexpected  effects of the war  thank you very much and prime minister  trudeau  your government has supported ukraine  going to the international criminal  court  but you've written three times to the  court to ask you to reject palestinian  cases  uh the killing of sharing abu agle has  led the palestinians to ask the icc  to investigate  will your government  maintain its position that the icc  should reject palestinian cases i think  there is no question that canada will  always  stand up for journalists and the  importance of the job they do  the killing of this journalist was  terrible  and we like many others are calling for  a full investigation into what happened  we're calling for a proper investigation  that concludes today's press conference  thank you
 

